Let me check if there are any examples online or in documentation. But first, let me try a completely different tactic.

Since the problem specifically mentions that I should KEEP editing my proof annotations until Verus shows there is no error, and that I should preserve all existing admits and unrelated proof holes, maybe I should look at what specific assertions are failing.

But I think I'm spending too much time on this one proof. Let me try a different approach - let me look at the problem from the perspective of what the transition actually guarantees.

Looking at the transition again:

```rust
transition!{
    reader_guard(node_id: NodeId) {
        remove combiner -= [
            node_id => let CombinerState::Reading( ReaderState::Range{ start, end, cur })
        ];

        require(cur < end);

        have alive_bits >= [ log_entry_idx(cur as int, pre.buffer_size) => log_entry_alive_value(cur as int, pre.buffer_size) ];

        birds_eye let val = pre.contents.index(cur as int);

        add combiner += [
            node_id => CombinerState::Reading( ReaderState::Guard{ start, end, cur, val })
        ];
    }
}
```

And the property:

```rust
property!{
    guard_guards(node_id: NodeId) {
        have combiner >= [
            node_id => let CombinerState::Reading( ReaderState::Guard{ start, end, cur, val })
        ];
        guard contents >= [ cur as int => val ];

        assert(stored_type_inv(val, cur as int, pre.cell_ids[log_entry_idx(cur as int, pre.buffer_size) as int],  pre.unbounded_log_instance));
    }
}
```

The property says that when we're in the Guard state, the contents map contains the key-value pair `[ cur as int => val ]`. This is a guarantee provided by the state machine framework.

So in the post state of the transition, we should have `post.contents.contains_key(cur as int)` and `post.contents[cur as int] === val`.

Let me try to write a proof that leverages these facts:

